WEBVTT

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.016
Journey thirteen: respond to customer requests.

00:00:04.016 --> 00:00:09.512
An owner sees the requests customers sent, and confirms or declines each

00:00:09.512 --> 00:00:14.397
one. This run takes the journey from ninety percent to complete.

00:00:15.254 --> 00:00:20.940
Naomi owns Luzandre.com. Incoming requests shows three new ones: two

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:25.790
orders and a quote request, each with Decline and Confirm.

00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:31.482
New in this run, the page listens to the event stream instead of asking

00:00:31.482 --> 00:00:36.544
every five seconds. Grace sends a booking request, and it is on the page

00:00:36.544 --> 00:00:39.427
at once, announced in one toast, not two.

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:45.651
Naomi confirms Samuel's order, and closes the dialog with Escape before

00:00:45.651 --> 00:00:49.850
the answer is back. This time the answer is a failure.

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:56.441
She is still told. A danger toast says: couldn't confirm the order.

00:00:56.441 --> 00:01:02.242
Samuel's request is still new. It stays until she dismisses it, or

00:01:02.242 --> 00:01:03.824
presses Try again.

00:01:04.527 --> 00:01:08.442
Try again confirms the same request from the page.

00:01:08.442 --> 00:01:13.767
Order confirmed, Samuel has been told, and Undo is offered as usual.

00:01:14.468 --> 00:01:19.165
The decline dialog now opens on Keep request, the safe choice.

00:01:19.165 --> 00:01:24.014
Naomi writes to David and declines, but David cancelled a moment

00:01:24.014 --> 00:01:24.620
earlier.

00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:30.669
So the dialog closes, the list is read again, and a toast says why:

00:01:30.669 --> 00:01:35.138
request not declined. David Chen cancelled this request.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.155
An owner who answers within a day keeps the Replies fast badge.

00:01:40.155 --> 00:01:44.951
Members now see it: on the listing page beside the credentials, and on

00:01:44.951 --> 00:01:46.184
the business card.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:52.686
Behind the screen. While the event stream cannot connect, the page asks

00:01:52.686 --> 00:01:57.306
every five seconds, and stops the moment the stream is back.

00:01:57.306 --> 00:02:00.155
While it is connected, no timer runs.

00:02:01.012 --> 00:02:05.516
The job that awards the badge now runs once per hour, however many

00:02:05.516 --> 00:02:08.723
instances are up, and records its last success.

00:02:08.723 --> 00:02:13.636
And the tests now assert that the customer is told of a confirmation and

00:02:13.636 --> 00:02:14.524
of a decline.

00:02:15.382 --> 00:02:20.533
Journey thirteen is complete. Left for the owner: the visual, responsive

00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:25.256
and accessibility sweeps, and the migration that adds the job runs

00:02:25.256 --> 00:02:30.265
table. Retries and the dead-letter queue belong to the background jobs

00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:30.766
design.
